Third-party material

OverTheDiff is built from Rust crates under their own licences. Cargo.lock pins every one, and
each crate's licence is in its manifest. Every crate's licence expression offers an option made only
of identifiers on the Craft licence allowlist (storytold/craft-contrib standards/licences.md). Two
crates also offer a GPL-family licence as an alternative, and OverTheDiff uses them under their
allowlisted options only:

- self_cell (Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only), through epaint: used under Apache-2.0.
- r-efi (MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR LGPL-2.1-or-later), through getrandom on UEFI targets only, which
  OverTheDiff doesn't build for: used under MIT OR Apache-2.0.

Regular expressions (crates/regex) run on fancy-regex (MIT), with regex-automata and regex-syntax
(MIT OR Apache-2.0), aho-corasick and memchr (Unlicense OR MIT), and bit-set and bit-vec
(Apache-2.0 OR MIT).

On macOS and Linux, the local data source looks up owner and group names with uzers (MIT,
Copyright (c) 2019 Benjamin Sago and 2023 Sandro-Alessio Gierens), which links libc (MIT OR Apache-2.0).
It asks which file system a path is on, and whether it is mounted read-only, with rustix and
linux-raw-sys (Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT), bitflags and errno (MIT OR
Apache-2.0) on macOS and Linux, and with windows-sys and windows-link (MIT OR Apache-2.0) on Windows.
On macOS it also asks libc whether a volume's names differ by case.

The launch hand-off (otd-launch) adds craft-ipc (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and its dependencies, among
them interprocess (0BSD OR Apache-2.0), recvmsg and doctest-file (0BSD), and generic-array and zmij
(MIT). The rest offer MIT OR Apache-2.0.

The local data source moves items to the OS recycle bin or trash with trash (MIT, Copyright 2019
Artúr Barnabás Kovács), which brings objc2 and objc2-foundation (MIT) on macOS, the windows crates
(MIT OR Apache-2.0) on Windows, and jiff (Unlicense OR MIT) and urlencoding (MIT) on Linux. It
writes files crash-safely with craft-atomic (MIT OR Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs) and asks
macOS and Linux which file system a path is on with rustix (Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR
Apache-2.0 OR MIT).

The tests also use egui_kittest, kittest, craft-testkit and tempfile (each MIT OR Apache-2.0),
which the app doesn't ship.

OverTheDiff bundles no third-party fonts, images or other assets yet. Each one added is listed
here with its notice, and in ATTRIBUTION.md.
